      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the four positive integers \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) such that when they are divided by 11, they give remainders 2, 4, 6, and 8 respectively, we can express these integers as follows:\n\\[\na = 11k + 2, \\quad b = 11m + 4, \\quad c = 11n + 6, \\quad d = 11p + 8\n\\]\nwhere \\(k, m, n,\\) and \\(p\\) are non-negative integers. The problem is to find the remainder when the sum of these integers, \\(a + b + c + d\\), is divided by 11.\n\nCalculate the sum:\n\\[\na + b + c + d = (11k + 2) + (11m + 4) + (11n + 6) + (11p + 8).\n\\]\nSimplify the sum by combining like terms:\n\\[\na + b + c + d = 11k + 11m + 11n + 11p + 2 + 4 + 6 + 8.\n\\]\n\nFactor out the common term \\(\\left(11(k + m + n + p)\\right)\\):\n\\[\na + b + c + d = 11(k + m + n + p) + 20.\n\\]\n\nNow observe the sum of 20 divided by 11:\n\\[\n20 = 11 \\times 1 + 9.\n\\]\nHence,\n\\[\n20 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}.\n\\]\n\nSince our expression \\(a + b + c + d\\) involves the term 11 times a non-negative integer in front of \\(k + m + n + p\\), the remainder of \\(a + b + c + d\\) by 11 stays unaffected by the multiples of 11:\n\\[\n(a + b + c + d) \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when the sum of the four integers is divided by 11 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
